ems can be removed without affecting rankings → \"so what?\"\n4. No direct empirical comparison with existing detectors (ConStat, DVD)\n5. Temporal cohort definition is coarse — release date conflated with many variables; DIF says \"that\" not \"why\" limits diagnostic utility\n6. GSM1K correlation underpowered (N=33) and over-interpreted\n7. Missing head-to-head with Growing Pains\n\n### R2's Questions\n1. Can you run ConStat on METABENCH matrix?\n2. What if using training data cutoff vs release date?\n3. Difficulty-direction interaction driven by ceiling/floor effects?\n4. Can the audit protocol be validated retrospectively on known problem items?\n5. Sensitivity to K=5 strata choice?\n6. Within-family Llama-2 vs Llama-3: training data overlap vs ability confounding?\n\n### R2's Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5, Excitement: 3/5, Reproducibility: 4/5, Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a 300-400 word response addressing:\n1. Where do you agree with R2?\n2. Where do you disagree?\n3. Does R2's review change your overall assessment? If so, how?\n4. What are the 2-3 most important issues the authors must address?\n\nBe concise and direct."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GeyeiQS3jPwoc8c9SiMQx4","session_id":"ce551a50-54ba-4aaa-8fff-25f5094463ff","uuid":"76a4eda0-6c7e-4c61-91d4-628be2724d87","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:10:08.272Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015joCyb7uiNonycivvH1Lgb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0196hUSAW7oyScS3qkBnxLvm","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 cross-review response","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), who previously reviewed an EMNLP 2026 paper on \"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis.\"\n\nYour original scores were:\n- Soundness: 4/5, Excitement: 3/5, Reproducibility: 4/5, Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence: 4/5\n\nYour key concerns were:\n1. Core method entirely borrowed — MH-DIF applied without modification\n2. Proposition 1 is trivially true\n3. \"Instability ≠ invalidity\" is self-limiting\n4. No direct ConStat/DVD comparison\n5. Coarse temporal cohort definition\n6. GSM1K correlation underpowered\n7. Missing head-to-head with Growing Pains\n\nNow read Reviewer 1's review and respond. Note areas of agreement and disagreement. Consider whether R1's points change your assessment.\n\n## R1's Review\n\n### Summary\nR1 notes the paper covers 5,227 models across 12,508 items with orders of magnitude more data than prior DIF work. Six distinct controls systematically rule out methodological confounds. Cross-subject matching is a genuine contribution. But multidimensionality and missing comparisons are concerns.\n\n### R1's Strengths\n1. Scale and thoroughness of controls — 6 distinct controls, convergence of matched C% around 28-29%\n2. Cross-subject matching is genuine methodological contribution — Proposition 1 is clean and correct, clear reuse value\n3. Appropriate hedging of causal claims\n4. Cross-benchmark replication strengthens main finding\n5. Semi-synthetic dose-response validation\n6. Honest reporting of failures\n\n### R1's Weaknesses\n1. **MH validity under multidimensionality (CFI=0.681)** — Total score is poor proxy for theta in 57-dimensional space. Within-subject C%=38.1% HIGHER than overall 31.3% is concerning. \"Removing cross-subject dependence structure increases detected DIF\" could mean total-score conditioning was masking artifacts.\n2. Semi-synthetic injection mechanism has limited ecological validity (uniform p, no heterogeneous schedules)\n3. Per-family stratified permutation not executed — family clusters create non-exchangeable labels\n4. Unexecuted ConStat/DVD comparisons\n5. Downstream impact circularity — no comparison against IRT-based discrimination filtering or scalability coefficient filtering\n6. Temporal cohort definition is coarse\n7. Wide bootstrap CI [29.4%, 38.1%]\n\n### R1's Key Questions\n1. Why does within-subject C% (38.1%) exceed overall temporal C% (31.3%)?\n2. Is difficulty alone sufficient to produce the Simpson's paradox OR reversal, or is discrimination also needed?\n3. Why is 6-month window C% (34.35%) higher than full-year (31.3%)?\n4. Item-level correlation between |Δ_MH| and item difficulty?\n5. Same model cohorts across all 6 benchmarks?\n6. How sensitive is ΔFPR=0 to cross-subject transfer effects?\n\n### R1's Scores\n- Soundness: 3.5/5, Excitement: 3.5/5, Reproducibility: 4/5, Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a 300-400 word response addressing:\n1. Where do you agree with R1?\n2. Where do you disagree?\n3. Does R1's review change your overall assessment? If so, how?\n4. What are the 2-3 most important issues the authors must address?\n\nBe concise and direct."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12643,"cache_read_input_tokens":48487,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12643},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ce551a50-54ba-4aaa-8fff-25f5094463ff","uuid":"c42bf106-9a9c-4894-9d49-8e634c2f12d9"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"acea8a513bab14439","tool_use_id":"toolu_0196hUSAW7oyScS3qkBnxLvm","description":"R2 cross-review response","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), who previously reviewed an EMNLP 2026 paper on \"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis.\"\n\nYour original scores were:\n- Soundness: 4/5, Excitement: 3/5, Reproducibility: 4/5, Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence: 4/5\n\nYour key concerns were:\n1. Core method entirely borrowed — MH-DIF applied without modification\n2. Proposition 1 is trivially true\n3. \"Instability ≠ invalidity\" is self-limiting\n4. No direct ConStat/DVD comparison\n5. Coarse temporal cohort definition\n6. GSM1K correlation underpowered\n7. Missing head-to-head with Growing Pains\n\nNow read Reviewer 1's review and respond. Note areas of agreement and disagreement. Consider whether R1's points change your assessment.\n\n## R1's Review\n\n### Summary\nR1 notes the paper covers 5,227 models across 12,508 items with orders of magnitude more data than prior DIF work. Six distinct controls systematically rule out methodological confounds. Cross-subject matching is a genuine contribution. But multidimensionality and missing comparisons are concerns.\n\n### R1's Strengths\n1. Scale and thoroughness of controls — 6 distinct controls, convergence of matched C% around 28-29%\n2. Cross-subject matching is genuine methodological contribution — Proposition 1 is clean and correct, clear reuse value\n3. Appropriate hedging of causal claims\n4. Cross-benchmark replication strengthens main finding\n5. Semi-synthetic dose-response validation\n6. Honest reporting of failures\n\n### R1's Weaknesses\n1. **MH validity under multidimensionality (CFI=0.681)** — Total score is poor proxy for theta in 57-dimensional space. Within-subject C%=38.1% HIGHER than overall 31.3% is concerning. \"Removing cross-subject dependence structure increases detected DIF\" could mean total-score conditioning was masking artifacts.\n2. Semi-synthetic injection mechanism has limited ecological validity (uniform p, no heterogeneous schedules)\n3. Per-family stratified permutation not executed — family clusters create non-exchangeable labels\n4. Unexecuted ConStat/DVD comparisons\n5. Downstream impact circularity — no comparison against IRT-based discrimination filtering or scalability coefficient filtering\n6. Temporal cohort definition is coarse\n7. Wide bootstrap CI [29.4%, 38.1%]\n\n### R1's Key Questions\n1. Why does within-subject C% (38.1%) exceed overall temporal C% (31.3%)?\n2. Is difficulty alone sufficient to produce the Simpson's paradox OR reversal, or is discrimination also needed?\n3. Why is 6-month window C% (34.35%) higher than full-year (31.3%)?\n4. Item-level correlation between |Δ_MH| and item difficulty?\n5. Same model cohorts across all 6 benchmarks?\n6. How sensitive is ΔFPR=0 to cross-subject transfer effects?\n\n### R1's Scores\n- Soundness: 3.5/5, Excitement: 3.5/5, Reproducibility: 4/5, Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a 300-400 word response addressing:\n1. Where do you agree with R1?\n2. Where do you disagree?\n3. Does R1's review change your overall assessment? If so, how?\n4. What are the 2-3 most important issues the authors must address?\n\nBe concise and direct.","uuid":"57853dd4-d08b-4f9c-b563-0ebd2b2ba849","session_id":"ce551a50-54ba-4aaa-8fff-25f5094463ff"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), who previously reviewed an EMNLP 2026 paper on \"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis.\"\n\nYour original scores were:\n- Soundness: 4/5, Excitement: 3/5, Reproducibility: 4/5, Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence: 4/5\n\nYour key concerns were:\n1. Core method entirely borrowed — MH-DIF applied without modification\n2. Proposition 1 is trivially true\n3. \"Instability ≠ invalidity\" is self-limiting\n4. No direct ConStat/DVD comparison\n5. Coarse temporal cohort definition\n6. GSM1K correlation underpowered\n7. Missing head-to-head with Growing Pains\n\nNow read Reviewer 1's review and respond. Note areas of agreement and disagreement. Consider whether R1's points change your assessment.\n\n## R1's Review\n\n### Summary\nR1 notes the paper covers 5,227 models across 12,508 items with orders of magnitude more data than prior DIF work. Six distinct controls systematically rule out methodological confounds. Cross-subject matching is a genuine contribution. But multidimensionality and missing comparisons are concerns.\n\n### R1's Strengths\n1. Scale and thoroughness of controls — 6 distinct controls, convergence of matched C% around 28-29%\n2. Cross-subject matching is genuine methodological contribution — Proposition 1 is clean and correct, clear reuse value\n3. Appropriate hedging of causal claims\n4. Cross-benchmark replication strengthens main finding\n5. Semi-synthetic dose-response validation\n6. Honest reporting of failures\n\n### R1's Weaknesses\n1. **MH validity under multidimensionality (CFI=0.681)** — Total score is poor proxy for theta in 57-dimensional space. Within-subject C%=38.1% HIGHER than overall 31.3% is concerning. \"Removing cross-subject dependence structure increases detected DIF\" could mean total-score conditioning was masking artifacts.\n2. Semi-synthetic injection mechanism has limited ecological validity (uniform p, no heterogeneous schedules)\n3. Per-family stratified permutation not executed — family clusters create non-exchangeable labels\n4. Unexecuted ConStat/DVD comparisons\n5. Downstream impact circularity — no comparison against IRT-based discrimination filtering or scalability coefficient filtering\n6. Temporal cohort definition is coarse\n7. Wide bootstrap CI [29.4%, 38.1%]\n\n### R1's Key Questions\n1. Why does within-subject C% (38.1%) exceed overall temporal C% (31.3%)?\n2. Is difficulty alone sufficient to produce the Simpson's paradox OR reversal, or is discrimination also needed?\n3. Why is 6-month window C% (34.35%) higher than full-year (31.3%)?\n4. Item-level correlation between |Δ_MH| and item difficulty?\n5. Same model cohorts across all 6 benchmarks?\n6. How sensitive is ΔFPR=0 to cross-subject transfer effects?\n\n### R1's Scores\n- Soundness: 3.5/5, Excitement: 3.5/5, Reproducibility: 4/5, Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a 300-400 word response addressing:\n1. Where do you agree with R1?\n2. Where do you disagree?\n3. Does R1's review change your overall assessment? If so, how?\n4. What are the 2-3 most important issues the authors must address?\n\nBe concise and direct."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0196hUSAW7oyScS3qkBnxLvm","session_id":"ce551a50-54ba-4aaa-8fff-25f5094463ff","uuid":"8972fa5e-33fe-4e5e-bdae-66e511a64ca8","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:10:23.697Z"}
