{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012TJJBxEftfrsW3RSd63yau","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0174ddcSk9x78QRjyW1K1jVq","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks\n\n**Paper:** \"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\"\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Summary\n\nTwo reviewers independently assessed the paper, then exchanged reviews and responded. Both converged on **Overall Assessment 3.5 (Borderline Conference)**. The roundtable identified three critical issues requiring revision and reached consensus on the paper's position: a well-executed empirical study with useful but incremental insights, currently better suited for Findings than Main.\n\n### Convergence Points\n- Both reviewers independently scored Overall 3.5\n- Three mandatory revision issues identified unanimously: (1) multidimensionality robustness, (2) direct ConStat/DVD comparison, (3) temporal window sensitivity\n- Simpson's paradox (OR reversal 1.55→0.73) recognized as the most novel analytical contribution\n- Cross-benchmark replication across 6 benchmarks is a genuine strength\n- Honest reporting of negative results (bifactor IRT failure, unexecuted experiments) is appreciated\n\n### Key Disagreements (Resolved)\n- **Proposition 1 (ΔFPR=0):** R1 initially viewed cross-subject matching as \"genuine methodological contribution\"; R2 called it \"trivially true.\" After exchange, R1 conceded Proposition 1 is overstated as a formal proposition, though the implementation has reuse value.\n- **Excitement:** R1 gave 3.5, R2 gave 3. R2 argued \"more rigorous measurement of a known phenomenon\" caps excitement at 3. Not fully resolved — averaged to 3.25.\n- **\"Instability ≠ Invalidity\":** R2 framed this as self-limiting (\"so what?\"); R1 defended it as actionable (maintainers can clean items without sacrificing discriminative power). Both agree the audit protocol needs concrete validation.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper applies MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items) and finds 31.3% temporal instability, replicated across 6 benchmarks. Controls are thorough and causal claims appropriately hedged, but the multidimensionality concern and missing direct comparisons with existing detectors prevent a clear accept recommendation.\n\n### Strengths\n1. **Scale and thoroughness of controls.** Six distinct controls (random split, ability-quintile, within-family, matched-ability, architecture, within-subject) systematically rule out confounds. The convergence of matched C% around 28–29% across different analyses is compelling.\n2. **Cross-subject matching is a genuine methodological contribution.** ΔFPR=0.000 with bootstrap confirmation has clear reuse value for semi-synthetic DIF evaluation in multi-domain benchmarks.\n3. **Appropriate hedging of causal claims.** GSM1K correlation labeled exploratory with wide CIs. Simpson's paradox presented as decomposition, not causal mechanism.\n4. **Cross-benchmark replication** across 6 benchmarks (C% 17.9–31.3%, all random controls <1%) substantially raises confidence this is not MMLU-specific.\n5. **Semi-synthetic dose-response validation.** Monotonic ΔTPR relationship with exploitation rate provides clean internal validity.\n6. **Honest reporting of failures.** Bifactor IRT convergence failure, underpowered GSM1K, and explicit listing of unexecuted experiments demonstrate scientific transparency.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **MH validity under severe multidimensionality (CRITICAL).** CFI=0.681 for MMLU IRT fit. Within-subject DIF C%=38.1% exceeds overall temporal C%=31.3% — removing cross-subject dependence structure *increases* detected DIF, suggesting total-score conditioning may mask multidimensionality artifacts rather than control for them. The ability-quintile control (C%=2.9%) does not address dimension-specific cohort differences.\n2. **Semi-synthetic injection has limited ecological validity.** Uniform exploitation rate p across all items/models is a crude model. Real contamination is heterogeneous — varies by item difficulty, model capability, and contamination mechanism. No heterogeneous injection schedules explored.\n3. **Per-family stratified permutation not executed.** Model families cluster by release date (2023=Llama-2 heavy, 2024=Llama-3 heavy). Simple label permutation may not preserve this structure. Graduated de-duplication (K=10: 22.7%, K=20: 29.1%) partially addresses but is not equivalent.\n4. **No direct ConStat/DVD empirical comparison.** Claims 2–3 implicitly position DIF against existing detectors, but the comparison is entirely conceptual. Running ConStat on the same METABENCH data is feasible and would be far more convincing.\n5. **Downstream impact circularity.** DIF removal compared only against random removal and accuracy-gap removal — no comparison against psychometrically principled alternatives (IRT-based discrimination filtering, scalability coefficient filtering).\n6. **Wide bootstrap CI [29.4%, 38.1%].** 9-percentage-point spread suggests substantial model-level heterogeneity in DIF patterns. Implications not discussed.\n7. **Coarse temporal cohort definition.** Calendar year conflates architecture, training data, alignment, and scale differences.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. Why does within-subject C% (38.1%) exceed overall temporal C% (31.3%)? Does total-score conditioning suppress or mask DIF signal?\n2. For Simpson's paradox: is item difficulty alone sufficient for the OR reversal, or is discrimination also needed? Could difficulty be a mediator rather than confounder?\n3. Why is 6-month window C% (34.35%) higher than full-year (31.3%)? Is this a sample size effect or non-monotonic temporal relationship?\n4. What is the item-level correlation between |Δ_MH| and item difficulty? Does DIF concentrate at difficulty extremes (floor/ceiling interaction)?\n5. Were the same model cohorts used across all 6 benchmarks?\n6. How sensitive is ΔFPR=0 to cross-subject transfer effects (training on subject X improving subject Y)?\n\n### Missing References\n- Wainer & Wang (2000) on testlet DIF — relevant to MMLU's subject-clustered structure\n- Penfield & Camilli (2007) DIF handbook chapter\n- Polo et al. (2025) on LLM benchmark reliability\n\n### Scores\n- **Soundness:** 3.5/5\n- **Excitement:** 3.5/5\n- **Reproducibility:** 4/5\n- **Overall Assessment:** 3.5 (Borderline Conference)\n- **Confidence:** 4/5\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper applies standard MH-DIF without modification to LLM benchmarks at unprecedented scale (5,227 models, 27,125 items across 6 benchmarks). Empirically thorough with useful conceptual contributions (ground truth meta-critique, Simpson's paradox), but core methodological novelty is limited and the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing is self-limiting.\n\n### Strengths\n1. **Scale of empirical analysis is unprecedented** for psychometric analysis of LLM benchmarks. Six alternative explanations systematically tested and ruled out.\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique is a genuine contribution.** The distinction between global exposure (web-overlap) and differential functioning (DIF), supported by enrichment≈1.0, Jaccard=0.206, phi=0.012, could influence how future contamination detectors are evaluated.\n3. **Simpson's paradox finding is the most novel contribution.** OR reversal from 1.55 to 0.73 after item-property conditioning is genuinely surprising and adds real insight.\n4. **Difficulty-direction interaction (ρ=−0.597)** — capability gains on hard items, erosion on easy items — suggests DIF captures genuine temporal construct shift.\n5. **Related work is comprehensive and well-structured.** Four clearly delineated areas with appropriate cross-disciplinary coverage.\n6. **Cross-benchmark two-tier pattern** (knowledge 27–31% vs skill 18–19%) is interpretable and adds structural insight.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **Core method is entirely borrowed.** MH-DIF (1959/1988) applied without modification. Paper states this explicitly. For EMNLP 2026, \"old method, new domain\" typically requires either non-trivial adaptation or transformative empirical findings. The findings are interesting but the paper itself argues they don't threaten validity.\n2. **Proposition 1 (ΔFPR=0) is trivially true.** Disjoint sets observation dressed as formal proposition inflates perceived methodological contribution. Cross-subject matching has psychometric precedent (Nandakumar 1993, Donoghue & Allen 1993).\n3. **\"Instability ≠ invalidity\" is self-limiting.** If 31% of items can be removed without affecting rankings, the practical significance is unclear. The audit protocol (§5) is generic and unvalidated.\n4. **No direct empirical comparison with ConStat/DVD.** Conceptual argument is logically sound but not as convincing as direct evidence on the same data.\n5. **Temporal cohort conflates many variables.** DIF says \"that\" items function differently, not \"why.\" Agnosticism about mechanism limits diagnostic utility for benchmark maintenance.\n6. **GSM1K correlation underpowered (N=33) and over-interpreted.** Wide CI [−0.71, −0.16] given substantial discussion space relative to evidential weight.\n7. **Missing head-to-head with Growing Pains.** Complementarity claim is conceptual, not demonstrated.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. Can you run ConStat on the METABENCH matrix and report item-level agreement with DIF flags?\n2. What is C% when using training data cutoff dates rather than model release dates?\n3. Is the difficulty-direction interaction (ρ=−0.597) driven by ceiling/floor effects?\n4. Can you demonstrate the audit protocol would have identified a known problem item retrospectively?\n5. How sensitive is 31% to K=5 strata choice? Results at K=10, K=20?\n6. Within-family Llama-2 vs Llama-3 (matched C%=28.3%): is residual DIF or residual confounding?\n\n### Missing References\n- Oren et al. (2024), \"Proving Test Set Contamination in Black Box Language Models\"\n- Deng et al. (2024) on benchmark contamination at scale\n- Penfield & Camilli (2007) DIF handbook chapter\n- Embretson & Reise (2000) or de Ayala (2009) for modern IRT treatment\n\n### Scores\n- **Soundness:** 4/5\n- **Excitement:** 3/5\n- **Reproducibility:** 4/5\n- **Overall Assessment:** 3.5 (Borderline Conference)\n- **Confidence:** 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Step 3: Cross-Review Exchange\n\n### R1 Response to R2\n**Agreements:** Missing ConStat/DVD comparison is critical (shared concern). Proposition 1 is overstated. GSM1K is underpowered. Simpson's paradox is the most novel finding.\n\n**Disagreements:** R2's \"old method, new domain needs transformative findings\" sets too high a bar — well-executed empirical work applying established psychometric tools to a domain that ignores them has value. R2's \"so what?\" dismissal of instability-invalidity message misreads the contribution — knowing 31% of items can be cleaned without sacrificing discriminative power is precisely actionable. R2's Soundness 4/5 seems generous given CFI=0.681 multidimensionality issue.\n\n**Assessment unchanged:** Maintains 3.5.\n\n### R2 Response to R1\n**Agreements:** R1's multidimensionality concern (CFI=0.681, within-subject C% > overall C%) is \"the strongest technical point in either review\" and was underweighted. Within-subject C%=38.1% exceeding overall 31.3% is \"genuinely troubling\" — could indicate collider-like artifacts from total-score conditioning. Wide bootstrap CI is fair point. ConStat/DVD comparison is mandatory.\n\n**Disagreements:** R1 overvalues cross-subject matching as \"genuine methodological contribution.\" Excitement 3.5 is too generous for \"more rigorous measurement of known phenomenon.\"\n\n**Assessment:** Maintains 3.5 but balance of reasons shifts — multidimensionality concern now looms larger than novelty concern.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Overall Score: 3.5 (Borderline Conference / Strong Findings)\n\n### Dimension Scores (Averaged)\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Avg |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Soundness | 3.5 | 4.0 | 3.75 |\n| Excitement | 3.5 | 3.0 | 3.25 |\n| Reproducibility | 4.0 | 4.0 | 4.0 |\n| Overall | 3.5 | 3.5 | 3.5 |\n\n### Three Mandatory Revision Issues (Consensus)\n\n**1. Multidimensionality Robustness (Critical — both reviewers, R2 upgraded priority after exchange)**\nCFI=0.681 is well below conventional unidimensionality thresholds. Within-subject C%=38.1% exceeding overall C%=31.3% suggests total-score conditioning may introduce artifacts in the 57-dimensional MMLU space. Authors must either:\n- Demonstrate robustness under simulation with CFI < 0.7\n- Apply a multidimensional IRT baseline\n- Provide convincing argument for why total-score conditioning remains valid\n\n**2. Direct ConStat/DVD Comparison (Critical — both reviewers independently)**\nClaims 2–3 implicitly position DIF against existing detectors but offer only conceptual comparison. Running ConStat on the same METABENCH matrix is feasible. Without direct item-level agreement statistics, the meta-critique remains theoretical. This is essentially a revision requirement.\n\n**3. Temporal Window Sensitivity (Important — both reviewers)**\nThe 6-month window yielding C%=34.35% (higher than full-year 31.3%) is counterintuitive and unexplained. Authors must clarify whether this reflects finer temporal signal, sample size effects, or evidence of non-temporal confounders. Multiple window analyses (3-month, 6-month, 12-month, 18-month) would strengthen robustness.\n\n### Additional Issues for Revision (Not Consensus-Critical but Recommended)\n\n4. **Per-family stratified permutation** — execute as supplementary analysis to address family-clustering confound\n5. **Heterogeneous semi-synthetic injection** — test non-uniform exploitation rates as function of item difficulty or model ability\n6. **Audit protocol validation** — demonstrate retrospectively on known problem items to move from prescriptive to validated\n7. **Downgrade Proposition 1** — rename from \"Proposition\" to \"Observation\" or \"Remark\" to avoid inflating methodological contribution\n8. **Tighten GSM1K discussion** — reduce discussion space to match evidential weight (N=33, wide CI)\n9. **Report standard errors for ΔTPR** across benchmarks and discuss bootstrap CI width implications\n\n### Path to Acceptance\n\n**For Findings (3.0–3.5):** The paper is close to Findings-ready. Addressing issues #1 (multidimensionality discussion/robustness check) and #3 (temporal window explanation) in a revision, plus softening Proposition 1 and tightening GSM1K discussion, would secure a Findings accept.\n\n**For Main Conference (≥4.0):** Requires all three mandatory issues resolved plus at least two additional issues. Specifically: (a) ConStat head-to-head, (b) multidimensionality simulation, (c) validated audit protocol case study, and (d) per-family stratified permutation. The novelty concern (R2's core objection) is harder to address without new methodological contributions.\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\nFrom the review_context.yaml experimental plan:\n- **Fully covered in paper:** plan_001 (core MMLU DIF), plan_004 (GSM8K), plan_011 (semi-synthetic), plan_028 (6-benchmark), plan_029 (permutation null), plan_030 (Simpson's paradox)\n- **Covered in appendix:** plan_002 (RL robustness), plan_007 (Bayesian DIF), plan_008 (graceful degradation), plan_009 (IFT), plan_010 (architecture)\n- **Reported as failed:** plan_005 (bifactor IRT — Appendix E)\n- **Reported as not executed:** plan_003a/b/c (ConStat, DVD, ensemble — Appendix F), plan_006 (item generation — Appendix F)\n- **Not in paper but in plan:** Per-family stratified permutation (plan_029 extension), within-subject DIF detail (plan_014), DIF-cleaned cohort demonstration (plan_032)\n\nThe paper covers the core experimental plan adequately. The main gaps (ConStat/DVD, per-family permutation) are acknowledged. The pivot from \"benchmark regeneration\" to \"diagnostic lens\" is well-executed given the bifactor IRT failure.\n\n### Reviewer Confidence and Calibration Notes\nBoth reviewers report Confidence 4/5. The convergence on 3.5 from independent assessments with different focal areas (methods vs. novelty) increases calibration confidence. The multidimensionality issue was identified by R1 and validated by R2 as the strongest technical concern — this cross-validation strengthens the finding. The novelty concern (unmodified method) is partially offset by the scale and thoroughness of the empirical work, but remains a barrier for Main conference acceptance.","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260521T141728","overall_score":"3.5 — Borderline Conference / Strong Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1348,"cache_read_input_tokens":64916,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1348},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ce551a50-54ba-4aaa-8fff-25f5094463ff","uuid":"a8e730c5-c94c-49a1-976d-3a5f1e7365c7"}
